1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:21,840
The signed rank test. Now this test is usually attributable to Wilcoxon, statistician Wilcoxon,

2
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:33,120
and it's nearly always used as a test for matched pair data. So we have data in matched pairs.

3
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:43,280
We'll clarify what that looks like presently. And as with a number of other methods we've looked at so far,

4
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:55,120
it's a non-parametric test in that it makes no explicit assumption about the nature of the

5
00:00:55,120 --> 00:01:03,040
distribution that gives rise to the matched pairs. Well, here we'll start with an example. And an

6
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:14,240
example is based here on assessing the mileage of a fuel with and without an additive, which is

7
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:22,960
designed to increase the mileage per gallon. So the data is as follows. There are 12 compact cars

8
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:49,760
cars. And their mileages were found with eight gallons of regular fuel. Let's call that R. And

9
00:01:49,760 --> 00:02:03,920
the same fuel with an additive. The additive being designed to, if anything, increase the miles per

10
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:13,840
gallon. Let's call that A. So what would our data look like in this case? Well, let's label our cars

11
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:29,040
one, two, three, four, and so on, up to 12. And for each car we have two mileages, one with the regular fuel

12
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:38,240
and one with the fuel with the additive. Well, the full data are included in the online notes. But let's say

13
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:49,680
for car one, it takes 142 miles for the eight gallons on the regular fuel and 144 with the additive.

14
00:02:50,560 --> 00:03:01,360
Car two actually did better with the regular fuel than with the additive. Car three 137 with the regular

15
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:15,920
150 with the additive. And let's say for the final car 134 miles on the regular and 146 with the additive.

16
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:27,360
So our data are in pairs. And that's a very important feature of the data in that we cannot assume

17
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:33,520
that let's say the observations on each car. So if we chose a particular car, let's say car three,

18
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:39,760
we couldn't assume that these observations 137 and 150 are independent of one another.

19
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:47,200
So we don't have two independent samples of size 12. We have 12 sets of pairs of observations.

20
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:57,280
Now, you have already met a test which considers paired data, and that's the paired t-test. And what do we do in the paired t-test?

21
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:04,960
Well, we look at the within-pair differences. And I'll call the within-pair differences D.

22
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:16,240
And I'll take the regular away from the additive. You could do it the other way around without any loss.

23
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:28,960
So here we have 2, here minus 8, 13, and so on, and 12 at the end here.

24
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:38,000
I'll write out the full set of differences later on. Now, these are taken to be observations

25
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:47,200
from some random variable, let's call it capital D. So that's the distribution

26
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:56,720
of the differences. And we have 12 observed differences. And our null hypothesis is that

27
00:04:56,720 --> 00:05:03,040
this random variable D, the distribution of the differences, is such that it's symmetric.

28
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:13,600
So it has a symmetric distribution about zero. So whatever it looks like, whatever the shape is,

29
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:18,960
it's symmetrically distributed about zero.

30
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:30,080
Our alternative could be just the negation of that. But here, in this particular example,

31
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:40,640
we consider that the only possible alternative to the null hypothesis would be that, well,

32
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:48,400
the additive increases mileage per gallon. And so the differences then would be expected,

33
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:53,280
because we're taking the regular value away from the additive value, we'd expect

34
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:58,960
more of these differences to be positive than negative. And so we'd expect

35
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:11,520
D to have a median greater than zero. So the median difference between the mileage on the additive

36
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:20,000
and the regular fuel, in the way that we've subtracted them here, is tending to be positive.

37
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:27,920
So that will be our null hypothesis here. And that's a one-sided case. Now let me just reiterate,

38
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:33,200
because this is often a source of confusion. Why do we sometimes take a one-sided? Why do we sometimes

39
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:38,640
take a two-sided? Well, we have to consider the context. And the context here is that the additive

40
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:45,680
to the fuel is expected, if it has any effect at all, to increase mileage, not decrease.

41
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:57,520
That seems to be the logical case to take. So let's consider the test in more generality before we return

42
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:04,880
to our example. So in general, we have n pairs of observations. So we have an x and a y

43
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:12,240
value paired together, and we've got n such pairs. What sort of circumstances do we have paired data?

44
00:07:12,240 --> 00:07:18,320
Well, it could be like the example in hand, where we have different measurements on the same individual

45
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:26,080
or the same item. It could be something like a before and an after test. So we apply a treatment

46
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:31,600
before and take a measurement. We apply a treatment afterwards and take a measurement. Or it could be

47
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:41,600
something like data on identical twins. So it's very important that when we have data in pairs, that we do

48
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:50,560
not overlook the fact because analyzing the data as if they were two independent samples will be nonsensical.

49
00:07:50,560 --> 00:08:06,800
So what do we do? Well, we find the differences. And let's take the x value away from the y value.

50
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:14,800
We could do it the other way around. It really doesn't matter. So we're going to have n differences.

51
00:08:14,800 --> 00:08:27,280
And we're going to rank the absolute value of these differences, smallest to largest.

52
00:08:27,280 --> 00:08:39,520
So the smallest one will have rank one, and the largest one will have rank n. And our test statistic

53
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:53,280
is, I'll denote it by t plus. It's the sum of the ranks of the differences

54
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:02,480
for which, let's say, the y value was greater than the x value,

55
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:09,360
which I could write, which I could write if I want to use some notation. I could write it as the sum

56
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:20,880
of all the pairs of t subscript i, where t subscript i takes the value 0, so it really doesn't count in

57
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:38,000
the sum if the y value were less than the x value. And it's the rank of the i-th difference if the y value

58
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:44,800
was greater than the x value. This begs the question, what happens if the y value and the x value do equal?

59
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:55,120
Well, the simple answer to that is we usually would simply remove that observation and reduce n by 1.

60
00:09:58,320 --> 00:10:04,560
So another hypothesis is that the difference is d is symmetric

61
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:16,320
symmetric about 0. And a necessary condition, sorry, rather a sufficient condition for that,

62
00:10:16,320 --> 00:10:22,160
is that the x and the y values each come from the same distribution, so they're identically distributed.

63
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:27,840
That's not actually necessary, but that would be a sufficient case. So that's a special case

64
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:37,360
which would make the distribution of the differences symmetric about 0. So when we're looking at the

65
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:43,680
the signs of the ranks, so we're looking at these ranks before we took absolute values,

66
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:52,640
well, we'd pretty much expect under another hypothesis that either positive or negative in sign

67
00:10:53,200 --> 00:11:01,040
so neither is equally likely. So neither the y value is expected to be bigger than the x value, nor the x value is

68
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:09,200
expected to be bigger than the y value. If we go back to our example, I'll work through this in some detail.

69
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:31,680
Let me write out the differences. The differences are 2, minus 8, 13, 7, minus 6, 5, 17, minus 3, 8, 10, 4, and 12.

70
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:49,440
And then, when I rank the absolute values, well, the smallest value is 2, in absolute terms the next value is the minus 3 there, forget about the sign,

71
00:11:49,440 --> 00:12:00,800
1, then that has, sorry, this has the next value, this has the next value, this the next one.

72
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:12,480
We have two 8s, a plus 8 and a minus 8, in absolute terms they're equal, we give them both rank

73
00:12:13,280 --> 00:12:20,160
seven and a half, because they're the seventh and eighth values in order, then we move on to the

74
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:30,000
ninth observation, which is 10, 13 has rank, sorry, 13 has rank 11, 12 has rank 10,

75
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:34,560
apologies for going out of order slightly, 17 has rank 12.

76
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:44,880
When we add up the sum of the positive ranks, so remember, that's a positive, that's a positive,

77
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:51,680
that's a positive, positive, positive, positive, positive, positive, positive, all but four of the

78
00:12:51,680 --> 00:13:02,240
observations, all but three of the observations actually were positive, then the sum of these ranks

79
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:13,280
comes to 63.5, and we ask the question, is that consistent with the null hypothesis

80
00:13:13,280 --> 00:13:17,840
that the ranks are from a distribution?

81
00:13:25,520 --> 00:13:28,160
Now, we have 12 pairs of observations,

82
00:13:28,160 --> 00:13:34,560
and in this case, it's not too difficult to completely enumerate

83
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:39,360
all the possible values of t

84
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:50,080
at least if we have access to a computer. So the smallest value would occur

85
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:59,280
in fact, when all of all of these values here were negative, because then when we're computing our

86
00:13:59,280 --> 00:14:04,880
test statistic, in fact, if the y value is always less than the x value,

87
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:14,000
all of the terms in this sum would be zero. And so the test statistic would be zero.

88
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:19,040
What's the largest value that the test statistic can take? Well, the largest value is when

89
00:14:19,760 --> 00:14:28,240
all of the ranks are of positive values, so all of the y values are bigger than the x values,

90
00:14:28,960 --> 00:14:40,800
and in that case, there would be 78 when we add up the numbers 1 to 12. And we can observe values

91
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:44,640
and so on, and so on, and so on in that range.

92
00:14:47,040 --> 00:14:50,480
Now, clearly, under the null hypothesis,

93
00:14:50,480 --> 00:15:00,240
not all of these values are equally likely, and there will be very little chance of observing

94
00:15:01,920 --> 00:15:06,880
all the y values being less than all the x values, not much chance of the test statistic being 1,

95
00:15:06,880 --> 00:15:17,200
and so on, and so on. So we would get a distribution, which looks normal-like, over the range of numbers,

96
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:24,880
0 to 78. Now we observed

97
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:34,160
the test statistic taking the value 63.5. So we have to ask the question,

98
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:42,000
what's the probability that the test statistic takes a value at least as extreme as that under the null hypothesis?

99
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:47,200
Now remember, we're here in a one-tailed case,

100
00:15:47,200 --> 00:16:00,240
because we are only considering that the additive can, if anything, increase the mileage per gallon,

101
00:16:00,240 --> 00:16:04,880
in which case the y values will be greater than the x values. We will not consider that there's

102
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:13,440
any possibility that the additive decreases the mileage. Well, via the software package R,

103
00:16:15,760 --> 00:16:23,200
we can discover that, in fact, the p-value of our test statistic, so the probability of observing

104
00:16:23,200 --> 00:16:37,040
the value of the test statistic t-plus as being 63.5 or bigger is 0.0271. So our p-value

105
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:49,760
is around 0.0271. And so at the 5% significance level,

106
00:16:55,600 --> 00:17:02,000
because our p-value is smaller than 0.05, there is quite strong evidence

107
00:17:07,040 --> 00:17:19,920
to reject the null hypothesis that the differences in the mileages on the regular fuel and the fuel

108
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:23,280
with the additive are symmetrically distributed about zero.

109
00:17:25,920 --> 00:17:32,240
Just a couple of points about the assigned rank test. We could have used a normal approximation.

110
00:17:32,240 --> 00:17:44,560
Why could we do that? Well, remember, our test statistic is a summation. So by the central limit theorem,

111
00:17:45,440 --> 00:17:52,720
sums and averages are approximately normally distributed, at least as the sample size,

112
00:17:52,720 --> 00:17:57,920
the number of terms in the sum grows large. The sample size isn't particularly large here,

113
00:17:57,920 --> 00:18:04,720
but if we didn't have a computer, it might have to do. So we could compute a normal approximation.

114
00:18:04,720 --> 00:18:08,960
The details of how you do that are in the notes. But actually, we have computers these days,

115
00:18:09,600 --> 00:18:15,360
and the use of a normal approximation is not so important.

116
00:18:17,920 --> 00:18:22,880
You might say, well, perhaps we could have used a paired t-test here. Maybe it would be reasonable to

117
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:32,000
assume that the differences are normally distributed. Unlike some non-parametric tests, the sidelamp test

118
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:39,040
actually compares well with its most commonly used parametric equivalent,

119
00:18:41,360 --> 00:18:43,840
even when the differences are normally distributed.

120
00:18:43,840 --> 00:19:00,400
If you're rather smart, and have thought carefully about the sign test, you might say,

121
00:19:00,400 --> 00:19:07,280
well, just a minute, couldn't we just look at our differences, as we have them here,

122
00:19:07,280 --> 00:19:16,240
and use the sign test on those. And the sign test would simply record the number of pluses and

123
00:19:16,240 --> 00:19:22,880
the number of minuses, and say, well, under the null hypothesis, each observation, each difference here,

124
00:19:22,880 --> 00:19:28,480
is equally likely to be positive and negative, and use the sign test. Now that's a valid test to use.

125
00:19:29,760 --> 00:19:36,480
Most often, this is inferior to using the sign rank test, because it fails to take into account

126
00:19:37,120 --> 00:19:43,840
the magnitude of the differences. So it fails to take into account how large the differences were.

127
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:53,280
And for example, we noticed that the three largest values, for example, 17, 13, and 12,

128
00:19:54,880 --> 00:20:00,400
in fact, the fourth largest value, were all positive. And the sign test would simply ignore

129
00:20:00,400 --> 00:20:04,960
that, and just be recording their sign. There are circumstances where, perhaps,

130
00:20:04,960 --> 00:20:11,360
we only want to incorporate the sign. But otherwise, I would say, the signed rank test

131
00:20:11,360 --> 00:20:17,920
is preferable to the sign test, and will have more power. That's the signed rank test,

132
00:20:17,920 --> 00:20:28,560
and I hope you've enjoyed it.

